WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.840
How does your ear know whether a tone is high or low?

2
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:08.470
Uncoil the cochlea.

3
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:12.110
Along it runs the basilar membrane, carrying the hair cells.

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.433
Near the base, where sound enters, it is narrow and stiff.

5
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:19.013
Toward the apex it is wide and floppy.

6
00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:25.017
So each place has a natural frequency, high where the membrane is stiff, low where

7
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.017
it is floppy.

8
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:30.717
Sound sets up a travelling wave that moves from the base toward the apex.

9
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.210
A high tone's wave slows and grows near its matching place, close to the base.

10
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:39.013
It peaks there, then dies away.

11
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:42.273
Now a low tone enters at the same base.

12
00:00:43.153 --> 00:00:44.153
Where will its wave peak?

13
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:54.743
Near the apex, where the membrane is floppy and its natural frequency is low.

14
00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:57.513
Frequency becomes position.

15
00:00:58.433 --> 00:00:59.713
This map is called tonotopy.

16
00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:05.903
Above about 1 kHz, each doubling of frequency takes nearly the same length of

17
00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:06.903
membrane.

18
00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:09.597
Georg von Békésy measured these waves.

19
00:01:10.497 --> 00:01:12.957
His peaks were far broader than a living ear's tuning.

20
00:01:14.017 --> 00:01:16.177
Something in a living ear adds energy.

21
00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:18.717
That something is the outer hair cells.

22
00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.090
They change length when their voltage changes.

23
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.390
The motor is a membrane protein called prestin.

24
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:31.320
Each outer hair cell is braced between the basilar membrane and the structures above

25
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.320
it.

26
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:37.490
Here is the loop. The membrane moves, and an outer hair cell's bundle is pushed.

27
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.220
Its voltage swings, and prestin shortens and lengthens the cell.

28
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.180
Its push is timed to go with the membrane's motion, like pushing a swing.

29
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.460
That adds energy near the peak.

30
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:52.473
The same switch, used as a motor.

31
00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:55.533
It sharpens tuning and raises sensitivity.

32
00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:58.923
The boost is largest for quiet sounds.

33
00:01:59.823 --> 00:02:04.523
Lose the outer hair cells, and the threshold of hearing rises: quiet sounds drop

34
00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:05.523
below it.

35
00:02:05.966 --> 00:02:10.466
If the inner hair cells and their nerve fibres still work, sounds above the new

36
00:02:10.466 --> 00:02:11.846
threshold are still heard.

37
00:02:12.706 --> 00:02:13.706
But tuning blurs.

38
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.510
Inner hair cells supply most of the auditory nerve.

39
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:19.350
They report the place.

40
00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.000
The brain reads pitch from where the active cells are.
